WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér. Or not quite. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.700
be more exact, I have had the honor to hold a talk&nbsp;
here at GTC, and today we are going to marvel at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:23.160
a seemingly impossible problem, and 4 miracle&nbsp;
papers from scientists at NVIDIA. Why these 4?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:29.100
Could these papers solve the impossible?&nbsp;
Well, we shall see together in a moment.&nbsp;

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.100
&nbsp;
If we wish to create a truly gorgeous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:37.800
photorealistic scene, in computer graphics,&nbsp;
we usually reach out to a light transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:46.680
simulation algorithm, and then, this happens.&nbsp;
Oh yes, concept number one. Noise! This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:54.540
not photorealistic at all, not yet anyway. Why is&nbsp;
that? Well, during this process, we have to shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.540 --> 00:01:01.080
millions and millions of light rays into the scene&nbsp;
to estimate how much light is bouncing around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:08.040
and before we have simulated enough rays, the&nbsp;
inaccuracies in our estimations show up as noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:15.300
in these images. This clears up over time, but it&nbsp;
may take from minutes to days for this to happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:24.480
even for a smaller scene. For instance, this one&nbsp;
took us 3 full weeks to finish. 3 weeks! Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.410
really. I am not kidding. Ouch.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:35.400
Solving this problem in real time seems absolutely&nbsp;
impossible, which has been the consensus in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:41.100
light transport research community for a long&nbsp;
while. So much so, that at the most prestigious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:47.220
computer graphics conference, SIGGRAPH, there&nbsp;
was even a course by the name: Ray tracing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:55.020
the future and ever will be. This was a bit of a&nbsp;
wordplay yes, but I hope you now have a feel of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.560
how impossible this problem feels.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:04.320
When I was starting out as a first year PhD&nbsp;
student, I was wondering whether real-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:11.340
light transport will be a possibility within&nbsp;
my lifetime. It was such an outrageous idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:19.800
I usually avoided even bringing up the question in&nbsp;
conversation. And boy, if only I knew what we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.560
going to be talking about today. Wow.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.760
So, we are still at the point where these images&nbsp;
take from hours to weeks to finish. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:36.960
I have good news and bad news. Let’s go with&nbsp;
the good news first. If you overhear some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.180
light transport researchers talking, this is&nbsp;
why you hear the phrase “importance sampling”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:48.540
a great deal. This means to choose where to&nbsp;
shoot these rays in the scene. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:55.680
you see one of those smart algorithms here,&nbsp;
called Metropolis Light Transport. This is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:02.220
of my favorites. It typically allocates these rays&nbsp;
much smarter than previous techniques, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:10.560
on difficult scenes. But, let’s go even smarter!&nbsp;
This is my other favorite Wenzel Jakob’s Manifold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:18.060
Exploration paper at work here. This algorithm is&nbsp;
absolutely incredible, and the way it develops an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.280
image over time is one of the most beautiful&nbsp;
sights in of all light transport research.&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.800
&nbsp;
So if we understand correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:33.000
the more complex these algorithms are, the smarter&nbsp;
they can get, however, at the same time, due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:40.080
their complexity, they cannot be implemented so&nbsp;
well on the graphics card. That is a big bummer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.150
So, what do we do?
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:46.800
Do we use a simpler algorithm and take&nbsp;
advantage of the ever-improving graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.920
cards in our machines, or write something&nbsp;
smarter and miss out on all of that?&nbsp;

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.520
&nbsp;
So now, I can’t believe I am saying this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:04.980
but let’s see how NVIDIA solved the impossible&nbsp;
through 4 amazing papers. And that is, how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:08.640
created real-time algorithms for light transport.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:17.400
Paper number one. Voxel Cone Tracing. Oh my, this&nbsp;
is an iconic paper that was one of the first signs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:26.820
of something bigger to come. Now, hold on to your&nbsp;
papers, and look at this. Oh my goodness. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:33.600
a beautiful, real-time light transport simulation&nbsp;
program. And it gets better, because this one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:43.140
paper that is from 2011, a more than 10-year-old&nbsp;
paper, and it could do all this. Wow! How is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:49.140
even possible? We just discussed that we’d be&nbsp;
lucky to have this in our lifetimes, and it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.140
that it was already here, 10 years ago!
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.140 --> 00:05:00.300
So, what is going on here? Is light transport&nbsp;
suddenly solved? Well, not quite. This solves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.940
not the full light transport simulation&nbsp;
problem, but it makes it a little simpler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:14.760
How? Well, it takes two big shortcuts. Shortcut&nbsp;
number one: it subdivides the space into voxels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:21.660
small little boxes, and it runs the light&nbsp;
simulation program on this reduced representation.&nbsp;

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.460
&nbsp;
Shortcut number two, it only computes 2 bounces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:34.440
for each light ray for the illumination. That is&nbsp;
pretty good, but not nearly as great as a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.400
solution with potentially infinitely many bounces.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:46.680
It also uses tons of memory, so, plenty of things&nbsp;
to improve here, but my goodness, if this was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:53.340
a quantum leap in light transport simulation, I&nbsp;
really don’t know what is. This really shows that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:59.760
scientists at NVIDIA are not afraid of completely&nbsp;
rethinking existing systems to make them better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:10.200
and boy, isn’t this a marvelous example of that.&nbsp;
And remember, all this in 2011. 2011! More than 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:18.480
years ago. Absolutely mind-blowing. And one more&nbsp;
thing: this is the culmination of software and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:25.080
hardware working together. Designing them for&nbsp;
each other. This would not have been possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:26.100
without it.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:32.640
But, once again, this is not the full light&nbsp;
transport. So, can we be a little more ambitious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:38.640
and hope for a real-time solution for&nbsp;
the full light transport problem? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:46.260
let’s have a look together and find out! And here&nbsp;
is where paper number two comes to the rescue. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:52.200
this newer work of theirs, they presented an&nbsp;
amusement park scene that contains a total of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.200 --> 00:07:00.660
over 20 million triangles, and it truly is&nbsp;
a sight to behold. So let’s see, and! Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:06.960
goodness! This does not take from minutes to days&nbsp;
to compute, each of these images were produced in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.900
a matter of milliseconds! Wow.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:18.420
And, it gets better. It can also render this&nbsp;
scene with 3.4 million light sources, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:25.200
method can really render not just an image, but&nbsp;
an animation of it interactively. What’s more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:32.760
the more detailed comparisons in the paper reveal&nbsp;
that this method is 10 to 100 times faster than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:40.680
previous techniques, and it also maps really well&nbsp;
onto our graphics cards. Okay, but what is behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.400
all this wizardry? How is this even possible?
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:51.000
Well, the magic behind all this is a smarter&nbsp;
allocation of these ray samples that we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:58.320
shoot into the scene. For instance, this technique&nbsp;
does not forget what we did just a moment ago when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:04.740
we move the camera a little and advance to the&nbsp;
next image. Thus, lots of information that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:12.120
otherwise thrown away can now be reused as we&nbsp;
advance the animation. Now note that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:18.180
so many papers out there on how to allocate&nbsp;
these rays properly, this field is so mature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:23.820
it truly is a challenge to create something&nbsp;
that is just a few percentage points better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:30.780
than previous techniques. It is very hard to&nbsp;
make even the tiniest difference. And to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:37.920
to create something that is 10 to a 100 times&nbsp;
better in this environment? That is insanity.&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:40.560
&nbsp;
And, this proper ray allocation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:48.660
has one more advantage. What is that? Well, have a&nbsp;
look at this. Imagine that you are a good painter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:55.440
and you are given this image. Now your task is&nbsp;
to finish it. Do you know what this depicts?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:04.500
Hmm…maybe. But knowing all the details of this&nbsp;
image is out of question. Now, look, we don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:11.880
to live with these noisy images, we have denoising&nbsp;
algorithms tailored for light simulations. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:18.120
one does some serious legwork with this noisy&nbsp;
input, but even this one cannot possibly know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:25.440
exactly what is going on because there is so much&nbsp;
information missing from the noisy input. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:32.040
if you have been holding on to your papers so far,&nbsp;
squeeze that paper, because, look. This technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:39.060
can produce this image in the same amount of&nbsp;
time. Now we’re talking! Now, let’s give it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:47.580
to the denoising algorithm, and…yes! We get a&nbsp;
much sharper, more detailed output. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:55.560
let’s compare it to the clean reference image.&nbsp;
Yes, yes yes! This is much closer. This really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.560 --> 00:10:01.380
blows my mind. We are now, one step closer&nbsp;
to proper, interactive light transport!&nbsp;

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:04.080
&nbsp;
Now note that I used the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:12.180
word interactively twice here. I did not say real&nbsp;
time. And that is not by mistake. These techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:18.960
are absolutely fantastic, one of the bigger leaps&nbsp;
in light transport research, but, they still cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:25.500
a little more than what production systems can&nbsp;
shoulder. They are not quite real time yet.&nbsp;

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.620
&nbsp;
And, I hope you know what’s coming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.360
Oh yes! Paper number three. Check this out!
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:39.240
This is their more recent result on the Paris&nbsp;
Opera House scene, which is quite detailed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:46.500
there is a ton going on here. And, you are all&nbsp;
experienced Fellow Scholars now, so when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:53.400
see them flicking between the raw, noisy and the&nbsp;
denoised results, you now know exactly what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.400 --> 00:11:00.900
going on. And, hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;
all this takes about 12 milliseconds per frame.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:10.320
That is over 80 frames per second. Yes yes yes! My&nbsp;
goodness! That is finally in the real time domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:13.560
and then some! What a time to be alive!
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:19.740
Okay, so where is the catch? Our keen&nbsp;
eyes see that this is a static scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:26.220
It probably can’t deal with dynamic movements&nbsp;
and rapid changes in lighting, can it? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:34.980
let’s have a look. Wow! I cannot believe my&nbsp;
eyes. Dynamic movement, checkmark. And here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:41.100
this is as much change in the lighting as&nbsp;
we would ever want, and it can do this too.&nbsp;

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:45.120
&nbsp;
And, we are still not done yet. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:53.340
real time is fantastic, I cannot overstate how&nbsp;
huge of an achievement that is. However, we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:59.640
a little more to make sure that the technique&nbsp;
works on a wide variety of practical cases.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:08.640
For instance, look here. Oh yes! That is a ton of&nbsp;
noise, and it’s not only noise, it is the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:16.980
kind of noise! High-frequency noise. The bane of&nbsp;
our existence. What does that mean? It means these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:23.340
bright fireflies. If you show that to a light&nbsp;
transport researcher, they will scream and run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:30.960
away. Why is that? It is because these are light&nbsp;
paths that are difficult to get to, and hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.380
take a ton more ray samples to clean up.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:41.640
And, you know what is coming! Of course, here&nbsp;
is paper number four. Let’s see what it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:49.980
do for us. Am I seeing correctly? That is so much&nbsp;
better! This seems nearly hopeless to clean up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:56.940
a reasonable amount of time, and this…this might&nbsp;
be ready to go as is with a good noise filter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:00.030
How cool is that!
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:05.520
Now, talking about difficult light paths,&nbsp;
let’s have a look at this beautiful caustics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:12.120
pattern here. Do you see it? Well, of course&nbsp;
you don’t! This region is so undersampled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:19.080
we not only can’t see it, it is hard to&nbsp;
even imagine what should be there. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:27.000
let’s see if this new method can accelerate&nbsp;
progress in this region. That is not true. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:33.960
just cannot be true. When I first saw this paper,&nbsp;
I could not believe this, and I had to recheck the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:41.940
results over and over again. This is at the very&nbsp;
least a 100 times more developed caustic pattern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:49.920
Once again, with a good noise filter, probably&nbsp;
ready to go as is. I absolutely love this one.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:57.300
Now, note that there are still shortcomings. None&nbsp;
of these techniques are perfect. Artifacts can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:03.780
still appear here and there, and around specular&nbsp;
and glossy reflections, things are still not as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:10.920
clear as the reference simulation. However, we now&nbsp;
have real time light transport and not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:18.000
but the direction we are heading to is truly&nbsp;
incredible, and amazing new papers are popping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:24.240
up what feels like every single month. Don’t&nbsp;
forget to apply The First Law Of Papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:31.200
which says that research is a process. Do not look&nbsp;
at where we are, look at where we will be two more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:32.820
papers down the line.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:38.760
Also, NVIDIA is amazing at democratizing&nbsp;
these tools and putting them into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:44.460
hands of everyone. Their tech transfer&nbsp;
track record is excellent. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:50.520
their marbles demo is already out there,&nbsp;
and not many know that they already have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:57.420
a denoising technique that is online and&nbsp;
ready to use for all of us. This one is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:02.520
professional grade tool right there. Many&nbsp;
of the papers that you have heard about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:10.560
today may see the same fate. So, real-time light&nbsp;
transport for all of us? Sign me up right now!&nbsp;

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:10.440
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

